3RD QUESTION

(a) System calls required to copy one file to another

The common system calls are:
open() – Open the source file.
creat() or open() (with create/write flags) – Create or open the destination file.
read() – Read data from the source file.
write() – Write data to the destination file.
close() – Close both files after the operation is complete.

(b) Purpose of any two system calls
* read()
Reads data from an open file into a memory buffer.
* write()
Writes data from a memory buffer to an open file.
Returns the number of bytes successfully written.

(c) Pseudo-code to copy the contents of one file to another
START
Open source file in READ mode
Create/Open destination file in WRITE mode
WHILE (data can be read from source file)
    Read data into buffer
    Write buffer to destination file
END WHILE
Close source file
Close destination file

4TH QUESTION 
(a) Purpose of fork() and exec() system calls
1. fork()
Creates a new child process by duplicating the calling (parent) process.
After fork(), both parent and child processes execute independently.
2. exec()
Replaces the current process image with a new program.
The process ID (PID) remains the same, but the old program is completely replaced by the new one.

(b) Role of the wait() system call
wait() causes the parent process to pause until one of its child processes finishes execution.
It allows the parent to collect the child's exit status.
It also prevents the child process from becoming a zombie process.

(c) System call sequence using fork(), exec(), and wait()

START
pid = fork()
IF (pid == 0) THEN
    // Child process
    exec("NewProgram")
ELSE
    // Parent process
    wait()
END IF
STOP

